WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.553 --> 00:00:08.970
WILLIAM DENNISS: Hello, and
welcome to our session "Deploy

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.060
Your Next Application to
Google Kubernetes Engine."

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.730
I'm William Dennis, a product
manager on Google Kubernetes

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.420
Engine.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.710
And later I'll be joined by
Marshall and Cat from Fair.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.210
In today's session
we're going to be

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.070
covering three broad topics.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.570
First, we're going
to talk about why

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.090
you might want to use Kubernetes
in the first place and some

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.062
of the benefits you can
expect to realize if you do.

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:32.520
Then I'm going to do a demo on
deploying an app into Google

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.525
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:35.400
And finally, we're going
to talk a little bit

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.050
about how to
introduce Kubernetes

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.170
into your enterprise.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.430
All right.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.320
So why should you use Kubernetes
as your application platform?

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.310
Well, first and
foremost, Kubernetes

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.330
is a container platform, so
you inherit all the benefits

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.030
of developing with containers.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.550
These include things
like language and library

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.740
flexibility, so
no longer are you

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.500
limited to a particular language
in your production environment,

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.342
like a certain version of Java
or a certain vision of Ruby,

00:01:04.342 --> 00:01:05.800
and no longer do
you have to put up

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.523
with some three-year-old
release of a library

00:01:08.523 --> 00:01:10.690
that for some reason is
still shipping in your Linux

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:11.920
distribution.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.390
With containers,
the developer is

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.550
in control of what
they want to run

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.900
and exactly how they
want to configure it.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.600
You also get isolated
dependencies,

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.850
so you can run multiple
containers on a single machine

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.500
without them interfering
with each other.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.845
And production and
development uniformity-- this

00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:31.372
is one of my favorite features.

00:01:31.372 --> 00:01:33.580
So your production environment
looks very, very close

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.990
to your development environment.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.920
And in fact, if you have
a bug in production,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.950
you can typically
download that container,

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.750
run it on your
developer machine,

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.060
attach to, say, the
staging endpoints,

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.500
and actually debug
the exact code

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.750
that was running in production.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.790
Not only that, but you
also get uniformity

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.112
between the developers
on your team.

00:01:51.112 --> 00:01:52.570
So gone are the
days where you have

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.970
to spend a whole day
configuring some development

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.090
machine with all the various
tools and things that you need.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.310
You just install Docker
and you can immediately

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.242
be building these containers.

00:02:02.242 --> 00:02:04.450
You can even have someone
on the team using a Windows

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.610
machine, someone
using a Linux machine,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.070
someone using Mac, all
developing and deploying

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.530
Linux containers, or
whatever operating system

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.060
that you wanted to use.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.190
And you get all this with
really minimal overhead

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.850
and complete resource isolation.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.180
So, in the past, you might
have used virtual machines

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.370
to get some of these benefits.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.530
By comparison, containers
are very lightweight,

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.070
both in the execution
and the low overhead

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:35.050
of the actual image size.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.200
So back to Kubernetes.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.460
Kubernetes is then
a container platform

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.100
that manages those
containers for you.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.980
It does things like handling
how to schedule and launch

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.720
the containers on the machines.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.090
It'll do things like reboot
any containers that crash.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.840
It can even run-- continually
monitor them with health check,

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.260
so that it won't route traffic
to something that's hung,

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.010
or a replica of your application
that, say, is missing

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.620
its connection to the database.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.500
It performs a lot of
operations like this,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.000
all with the goal of keeping
your application up and running

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.470
without too much help from you.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.380
So Kubernetes is a
workload level abstraction.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.660
And what I mean by that is
you describe your workload

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.160
and it basically has the
job of running that for you.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.280
So for example,
let's say you have

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.040
a server that you want to
run in a distributed way,

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.490
or you have a database
that has a disk attached

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.290
that needs to follow
it around, or maybe you

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:35.063
want to have a logging utility
that runs on every single node,

00:03:35.063 --> 00:03:36.480
or maybe you have
a movie that you

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.000
want to render one
frame at a time using

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.200
the cheapest possible compute.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.990
These are all
different workloads,

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.740
and these are all
things that you can just

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:48.262
represent in Kubernetes terms,
and it can handle it for you.

00:03:48.262 --> 00:03:50.470
And I believe that Kubernetes
sits at the right level

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:51.710
of abstraction.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.560
It's not super low
level in the sense

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.852
that you're not having to
deal with individual machines

00:03:55.852 --> 00:03:58.120
anymore, but it's not too
high level in the sense

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.162
that it doesn't interfere
with what you're trying

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:01.330
to do with your application.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.890
It kind of sits very
cleanly in the middle.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:07.373
And for me this contrasts
to traditional PaaS,

00:04:07.373 --> 00:04:09.540
which kind of like merges
into the application layer

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.630
by imposing a lot of
restrictions on you,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.760
and also occasionally
even modifying

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.440
how the code actually runs.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.365
With Kubernetes, these layers
between the infrastructure,

00:04:19.365 --> 00:04:21.240
the application platform,
and the application

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.470
itself are very, very clean.

00:04:24.938 --> 00:04:26.980
And I think that's important,
because when you're

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.780
choosing the application
platform you want to use

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.230
and when you're looking
at abstraction layers,

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.620
you want to prioritize making
hard tasks possible over making

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.880
simple tasks easier.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.430
So Kubernetes, instead of
focusing on the easiest

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.070
type of application to
deploy, which is typically,

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.480
say, a stateless application,
and making that task easier,

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.280
it actually focuses
on making it possible

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.980
for you to deploy a really
wide range of far more

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:56.600
complex deployments, things
like machine learning batch task

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.430
or a database that has state.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:02.520
And it can do all
this while enabling

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.998
a lot of really nice cost
efficiencies as well.

00:05:04.998 --> 00:05:06.540
So I mentioned before
that containers

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.790
have really low overhead.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.160
When you combine that
property of containers

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.890
with the power of Kubernetes
to manage those containers

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.350
for you, you can actually
really pack a whole bunch

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.540
of containers onto
machines, and you

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.220
can have a bunch
of machines running

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.617
at really high efficiency.

00:05:22.617 --> 00:05:24.200
And you can even do
that in such a way

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.870
that it doesn't compromise the
availability and reliability

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:27.740
of your services.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.240
And this is really
something I think

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.960
that's unique to Kubernetes
and one of the real big value

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.640
adds that Kubernetes brings
over just raw containers.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.710
Kubernetes also comes with a
rich ecosystem and community.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.760
So let's say you want to add
some logging or monitoring

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.790
service to your application,
with Kubernetes you typically

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:48.550
have the choice.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.390
You can either get some open
source and run it yourself

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.760
or you can find a vendor
that can manage and run

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.757
that for you.

00:05:54.757 --> 00:05:56.340
I think this is
really good because it

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.710
kind of lets you pick and choose
what you want to focus on.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.290
Maybe in some areas you'd rather
have someone else manage it

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:01.813
for you.

00:06:01.813 --> 00:06:03.480
But in others, maybe
you actually really

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:04.840
want to understand
how that's working

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.210
and you want to run it yourself.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.495
With Kubernetes, you
always have both options,

00:06:08.495 --> 00:06:09.870
I find, with pretty
much anything

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.570
that you want to try and do.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.230
And, of course, Kubernetes
itself is open source

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.890
and, perhaps
equally importantly,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.890
it's really widely available.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.690
How wide?

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.120
Well, if you look at
the certified Kubernetes

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.592
program run by the CNCF,
The Cloud Native Compute

00:06:26.592 --> 00:06:28.550
Foundation, you'll see
that there are currently

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.630
32 platforms that are
certified, 50 for distributions

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.160
and 14 installs.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.430
So you really have a wide
range of choice of where you

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.273
want to run that Kubernetes.

00:06:39.273 --> 00:06:40.940
And, of course, Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:45.250
is a founding member of the
certified Kubernetes program.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.260
And certainly, not all
Kubernetes platforms

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:49.200
are created equal.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.620
Kubernetes Engine was one of the
very earliest to go to market,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.170
the earliest managed
Kubernetes product.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.410
And we have a lot of really
robust automation capabilities,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.330
things like cluster
autoscaling, which

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.690
can add compute resources
when you need them

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.460
and remove them in
kind of a quiet period,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.258
so you can save some money.

00:07:07.258 --> 00:07:09.050
We have things like
node auto repair, which

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.900
can monitor various
unhealthy conditions in nodes

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.180
and automatically replace them.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.490
And we have node
auto upgrade, which

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:19.190
allows you to stay up to
date with the latest features

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.630
and security patches without
you needing to lift a finger.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.030
Together, these three
features and more

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.640
really help automate a lot
of the operations for you.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.270
And it basically means less
3:00 AM phone calls for you.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.380
And perhaps importantly--
equally importantly,

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.420
I think, that the same team
that brings you GKE, actually

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:43.450
contribute over 40%
to Kubernetes itself.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:48.180
So my team's contributions are
the kind of yellow orange line

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.340
at the bottom there.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.860
And you can see that, not
only do we contribute 40%,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.660
but we've actually
really sustained

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.760
and had a long term
commitment to Kubernetes.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.480
This graph goes back
nearly five years.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.270
And what this means
for you is that,

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.490
if you hit some bug in
Kubernetes or you're

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.478
having difficulty with something
that you just can't figure out,

00:08:07.478 --> 00:08:09.020
well, I'm pretty
sure there's someone

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.030
on my team who's going
to be able to help you

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.760
with that problem, probably
the person that actually wrote

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.010
the feature that you're using.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.970
And, of course, you get
all this with the benefits

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.230
of Google Cloud.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.600
So all the benefits that
you're hearing about this week

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.720
and that you will hear about
this week, such as our world

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:31.130
class fiber network, you
inherit all these benefits

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.159
on Google Kubernetes
Engine from Google Cloud.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:37.523
I really like this network.

00:08:37.523 --> 00:08:41.353
With this you're basically
getting the benefits of YouTube

00:08:41.353 --> 00:08:42.020
in a way, right?

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.480
Google built this thing
out to handle YouTube

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.730
and your little app can run on
the exact same fiber network.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.180
And we're even deploying
undersea cables now.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.950
So, again, all the
benefits of Google

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.640
Cloud in Kubernetes Engine.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:00.150
And speaking of scale, GKE can
definitely handle your scale.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.990
So I think this is really
important, actually,

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:05.262
that when you're designing
in your application,

00:09:05.262 --> 00:09:07.220
that you really want to
think about scalability

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.790
from the start.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:13.110
Unless you're creating some
internal only like lunch

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.680
ordering app, I'm sure
you want more users

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.740
and you want more scale.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.900
But imagine if at that
very moment when everyone's

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:21.100
kind of beating
down the door trying

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.017
to get into your products,
at that very moment

00:09:23.017 --> 00:09:26.225
of potential success, your
application falls over.

00:09:26.225 --> 00:09:27.100
That's just terrible.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.190
That's just the worst thing.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.440
So I believe if
you start with GKE,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.480
and as long as you follow some
good scalability principles,

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.190
we can be there for you and
ready to handle your scale

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.320
and set you up for success.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:42.880
A great example
of such a success

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.220
is Niantic when they
launched Pokemon.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:48.688
You might have heard of this
app last year, or two years ago.

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:50.230
And when they
launched, they actually

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.350
had the first day traffic, or
the first kind of week traffic,

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:56.560
was something in order of 50
times their original projection

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.365
and 10 times their worst
case projection of traffic.

00:09:59.365 --> 00:10:02.770
We were still able
to handle that.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:07.430
And, as you may know, the
launch was fairly successful.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.313
So another thing
about Kubernetes

00:10:09.313 --> 00:10:10.730
is it doesn't have
a very strongly

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:13.935
opinionated development
deployment workflow.

00:10:13.935 --> 00:10:16.310
So you might think that's a
burden, in the sense that you

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.920
do have to actually set up
your own deployment workflow.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:20.660
But, fortunately, using
something like Cloud Run,

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.535
you can actually get a
deployment workflow up

00:10:22.535 --> 00:10:23.370
pretty quickly.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.245
And the real power comes
in when you actually

00:10:25.245 --> 00:10:28.813
need to set up some business
logic for your enterprise.

00:10:28.813 --> 00:10:30.980
This might be something
like a vulnerability scanner

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.898
that you want to run
before every deploy

00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:34.940
to ensure that no
vulnerabilities are getting out

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.320
into the wild.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.420
Or perhaps it's just some
other admission control

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.102
that you just want to
check some behavior,

00:10:41.102 --> 00:10:43.310
enforce something to make
sure your developers aren't

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.510
doing the wrong thing.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.570
All these kind of
custom business logics

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:48.800
are possible for you to
set up in Kubernetes Engine

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.150
with your own custom pipeline.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.030
And with that, I'd actually like
to invite up Marshall and Kat

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.260
from Fair--

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.587
have a lot to say
about Kubernetes.

