00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.360
so there's no time commitment.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.270
The entire thing is
a mobile experience.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.580
You sign and pay for
the car on the app.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:43.328
If you're interested, check
it out in the app store.

00:11:43.328 --> 00:11:45.620
So I'm sure a lot of us has
gone through the experience

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.390
of buying a car.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:50.150
And you've probably realized
that it's not really

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.330
a fun experience.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.390
It's pretty outdated,
it's disjointed,

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:56.750
and there's not really
automation about it.

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:57.920
There's haggling involved.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:59.432
You got to get
your own financing.

00:11:59.432 --> 00:12:00.515
You have to get insurance.

00:12:00.515 --> 00:12:04.880
And at the end, there's a ton
of physical paperwork to sign.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.980
Every single part
of that process

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:10.370
can be its own business, and
usually is its own business.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:12.680
Fair's decided that we're
going to try and abstract all

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:14.840
that into a single experience.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:17.180
And that's what we've
done, but unfortunately

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:20.240
that brings a lot of legal,
financial and operational

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:21.630
complexities.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.690
So kind of from
the start, we said,

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:25.430
maybe a service
or an architecture

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:27.290
is going to be a good
fit for us as we try

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:31.043
and build out a business
that literally didn't exist.

00:12:31.043 --> 00:12:32.960
It's actually kind of a
funny story with that.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:34.687
Two weeks before we
launched we decided,

00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:36.770
we're going to add insurance
as a product on that.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.290
And we were able to do
that because of the way

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:41.820
that we had structured
our services.

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.260
But with a lot of
services, comes complexity,

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:47.340
and you need to manage
that complexity somehow.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:51.660
And that's kind of where
we chose Kubernetes.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.270
But how did we actually
end up on Kubernetes?

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.830
We didn't start there.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:13:00.120
The early Fair employees, we had
experiences at prior companies

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:05.130
with past products, and we knew
them, so we started with those.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.020
And it was working,
but it really

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:10.710
only solved one part of the
deployment process, which

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:11.860
is really just--

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:16.500
or not process, but solution
for API servers, web servers,

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.060
background job kind of things.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.130
It solved those.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.280
But our data
scientist team, they

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.158
had really sub-par
infrastructure.

00:13:24.158 --> 00:13:25.700
They were coming
from a prior company

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:28.520
where their training
was actually

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:33.070
done on a fixed set of VMs
and some in-office hardware.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:35.400
So it wasn't flexible
and it wasn't scalable.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.400
The way they actually did
their training is they

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.940
had to SSH into a box,
pull down their model code,

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.970
run the training code, and
then check back in on it later.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.470
So they were actually
kind of having

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:49.370
to coordinate leasing all of
that hardware among themselves

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:51.677
in order to get
their training done.

00:13:51.677 --> 00:13:53.510
So when coming to Fair,
they said, OK, we're

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:55.600
not going to bring all
that baggage with us,

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:56.975
we're going to
try something new.

00:13:56.975 --> 00:13:58.517
And they were
experimenting with ways

00:13:58.517 --> 00:14:00.230
of expressing their
training using kind

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.218
of like directed workflows.

00:14:02.218 --> 00:14:04.010
But they needed something
to actually power

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.343
that system, they needed
some infrastructure there.

00:14:06.343 --> 00:14:07.760
So that's kind of
where I came in.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.470
I was like, OK, you
need this problem

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.410
solved, let me go look
around and find some things

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.900
in the solution space.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.790
I was looking for something that
was relatively simple to use

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.220
and setup and could give
them on demand capacity.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.570
So when they needed to
submit their training jobs,

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.310
we could scale up, and when they
didn't have anything to train,

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.260
we could scale down, so
we weren't paying cost

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:29.030
on that hardware.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:30.910
Pretty quickly we
found Kubernetes.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:35.450
And that was really back in
the Kubernetes 1.1, 1.2 days.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:36.620
And took a few weeks.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.270
We integrated it
with their systems.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.680
And we got it working.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:41.220
And it was a huge success.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.600
It gave them all the
flexibility that they needed.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.476
And after a few more
weeks, we realized,

00:14:45.476 --> 00:14:48.722
wait, the workloads that we're
running here for their data

00:14:48.722 --> 00:14:50.180
science training,
we could actually

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:53.450
be running all of our workloads
on Kubernetes, the API servers,

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.940
background jobs, et cetera.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:57.837
So we cut everything over.

00:14:57.837 --> 00:15:00.170
And honestly, our devs were
actually a little resistant,

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.560
because their previous
past products they were

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.530
using, honestly, were simpler.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.150
But at the end of
the day, the ability

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.880
to actually have all of
our workloads running

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:14.120
on a single platform with
a single set of tooling,

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:17.720
that kind of operational
simplicity, really won out.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.330
And we started pretty
small and simple

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.540
with just a couple of
services, but we've grown.

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:24.650
We're not a Google,
but we're running

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:27.172
40-some odd various
kind of workloads.

00:15:27.172 --> 00:15:29.630
And Kubernetes has always been
able to run every single one

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:30.297
of those for us.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:33.780
CATHERINE CAI: So
we've been running

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.507
Kubernetes for two years.

00:15:35.507 --> 00:15:37.590
What are some of the
benefits we've actually seen?

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.230
Well, workflow customization.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.830
So William actually mentioned in
his portion of the presentation

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:46.860
that Kubernetes has really
solid primitives for expressing

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:48.228
different workload types.

00:15:48.228 --> 00:15:49.770
And we were actually
able to leverage

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:53.020
that to write a lot of tooling
to automate and abstract

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.720
our deployment workflow.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.890
So what does that mean?

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.490
Well, we created these
standardized build and release

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:02.520
pipelines that simplify
how an application goes

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.090
from source to production.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:07.680
So our developers actually can
spin up an entirely new service

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:11.070
and put it into production
and our platform team

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.710
does not have to be involved
with the process, which

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:15.960
is amazing for someone
like me, because I

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.570
don't want to spend all day
dealing with a developer

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:22.530
and helping them put their
app into production, right?

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.260
So how do we actually do this?

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:28.540
Well, it actually assumes
a lot of defaults,

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:30.690
but we actually give
developers the flexibility

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.150
to override some of the
default in our pipelines

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.290
to fit their specific needs.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:37.710
So what a data
scientist might actually

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:41.010
need to deploy their app
is very different from what

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.280
a regular app
developer needs, right?

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.060
And my point really is that,
even though our pipeline needs

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.160
have changed over time--

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.980
in two years Fair
has grown a lot,

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.040
Kubernetes has always been
underneath, very flexible,

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:57.390
and been able to fit our needs.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:03.800
So the next benefit
we actually got was it

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:06.540
forced us into very good
operational patterns.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:10.640
So we don't have any apps that
are pets or special snowflakes.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:13.790
And if you're not familiar with
the pets versus cattle analogy,

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:16.270
I'll spell it out
for you very quickly.

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:19.579
A pet is that one box that
you have in production

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:23.010
that you have to give a lot
of love, and if it goes down,

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.920
you're really screwed.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:29.000
A cattle is more the box
that you can terminate it

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.730
and there's probably another one
that's already running or will

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:35.540
come back up very quickly, so
you don't have a huge problem

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.633
if it's down in prod.

00:17:37.633 --> 00:17:39.050
So that's the way
that we actually

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:40.790
think of our applications.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:42.380
We run our applications
to try and be

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.190
as stateless as possible.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:47.030
And the reason for that
is because Kubernetes

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:48.890
can terminate your
workload at any time

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:51.470
and actually shift
it onto another node.

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:54.470
So, for example, your
node might actually

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.360
be suffering from
something critical,

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:01.100
like it might have very high
disk utilization or high CPU

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:04.520
utilization, so it might
actually terminate your app

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:06.920
and then move it
onto another node.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:11.030
So as a result, the way that we
think about our applications,

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:12.380
it's stateless.

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:15.073
We try and run multiple
replicas of them.

00:18:15.073 --> 00:18:16.490
And most of our
apps actually need

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:20.150
to be resilient to running
multiple versions at a time

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:24.170
because Kubernetes does
rolling deployments.

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:26.870
One other really awesome
benefit we got out of Kubernetes

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.460
was standardized
application visibility.

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.060
So with Kubernetes you
can see all of your app

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:34.040
logs, all of your app metrics.

00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:35.270
They're all in one place.

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:37.760
And on top of that, you can
see what apps are actually

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:39.720
deploying to the cluster.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.080
So with all of that,
we actually got

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.830
some really cool hidden benefits
out of Kubernetes as well.

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:49.940
We actually migrated our entire
workload into another cloud

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.490
onto GKE without any trouble.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:57.200
So it comes to my next
point, and the long list

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:00.260
of things you're probably
considering when you're

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:01.730
thinking about
adopting Kubernetes

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:05.860
is whether or not to go
managed or unmanaged.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:11.270
We're at a Google conference, so
really GKE or unmanaged, right?

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:14.660
So we actually started
on AWS in 2016.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:19.700
And we actually didn't have
any managed options on AWS--

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:23.570
EKS, which is Amazon's
equivalent of GKE,

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:26.240
didn't come out until late 2017.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:29.000
So we actually opted to
run unmanaged because that

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:30.960
was the only choice.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.170
And we've been running
it for two years

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:35.180
and we've learned a
lot about Kubernetes.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:38.300
So we've developed a lot of
really good in-house expertise

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:39.530
on Kubernetes.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:41.960
But also, after two
years, we realize

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:45.740
it's actually a full-time
job to run your machines,

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:49.610
it's a lot of security patches,
upgrading your machines.

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:52.700
And I know that, for a
lot of people in ops,

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.860
you're kind of
worried about going--

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.320
you want to go unmanaged
because you don't want

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:59.810
to lose operational control.

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:01.680
I'm just going to
say, take it from us,

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:03.480
we've been doing
this for two years.

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:06.530
And when we migrated
our workloads to GKE,

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.410
we didn't necessarily feel like
we were trading off or giving

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:11.000
up a lot of control.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:14.930
And more importantly, Kubernetes
is such a great abstraction

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:18.590
on top of cloud providers
that we transitioned and moved

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:21.260
all of our apps from
AWS to GKE and we

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:23.940
didn't have to make any
application changes.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:26.465
But speaking of apps and
deployments, Williams

00:20:26.465 --> 00:20:28.340
going to come back on
stage and he's actually

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:33.824
going to demo how to deploy
your app into Kubernetes.

00:20:33.824 --> 00:20:37.317
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:39.465 --> 00:20:40.590
WILLIAM DENNISS: All right.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.173
So before I get
started with the demo,

00:20:42.173 --> 00:20:45.415
I just wanted to quickly cover a
few key Kubernetes concepts, so

00:20:45.415 --> 00:20:47.540
that some of the stuff in
the demo will make sense,

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.230
just if you're
new to Kubernetes.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.240
So we'll start with containers.

00:20:53.835 --> 00:20:55.460
Kubernetes actually
wraps containers up

00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:57.210
into what's called a pod.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:01.890
So a pod is, for Kubernetes,
the smallest schedulable unit,

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:05.240
meaning that these containers
get deployed together.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:07.602
So maybe you'll have just
one container in a pod

00:21:07.602 --> 00:21:09.560
or maybe you have multiple
in the case you have

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.290
like a sidecar container that
adds like logging or monitoring

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:13.706
or something like that.

00:21:13.706 --> 00:21:15.840
So that's a pod.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:17.405
Then we have a
concept called nodes.

00:21:17.405 --> 00:21:18.780
Nodes are really
just a fancy way

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:24.270
of saying a machine, which
is either a VM or bare metal.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.940
And the real workhorse
in Kubernetes

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.350
is a concept called
a deployment.

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:33.700
And Kubernetes works on this
kind of operative pattern

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.417
where you just you
specify how many

00:21:36.417 --> 00:21:37.750
pods you want in the deployment.

00:21:37.750 --> 00:21:42.060
So you say like, I want two
of pod A and one of pod B.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.200
And then Kubernetes has
this operative pattern that

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:47.610
then seeks to drive the actual
observed state and the cost

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.580
to be the required state
that you specified.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.400
So that looks like
something like this.

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.300
So say we have a
system, and we have

00:21:55.300 --> 00:22:00.640
two of pod A, one of pod B.
And then, if one of those nodes

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.440
were to become
unhealthy and disappear,

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:06.130
the system would very quickly
notice, oh, wait a minute,

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:10.122
there's only like one of pod A
running now and none of pod B.

00:22:10.122 --> 00:22:11.580
It's going to then
reschedule those

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.580
onto another node, at
which time it will then

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.290
observe that the desired
state has been met

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:19.553
and everything is right
in the world again.

00:22:19.553 --> 00:22:20.970
So that all happens
automatically.

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:23.153
That's one of the very nice
things about Kubernetes.

00:22:23.153 --> 00:22:24.570
And that operative
pattern applies

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.490
to various other
constructs as well.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:28.950
Lastly, we have a service.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.350
And so a service is just
a group of identical pods

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:35.960
that together kind of provide
a logical API service.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:36.460
All right.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:40.670
And with that, I'm
going to go to the demo.

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:41.530
OK.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:45.850
So I was going to do like a
fairly boring hello world app,

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:47.850
but I decided on
Friday, actually,

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:50.930
to make something a
little more interesting.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:54.490
So I wrote a little
server in Swift.

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:58.630
And this server is actually
using this other little project

00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:01.420
at Google called Plus Codes
to convert a geographical

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:03.400
coordinate into a plus code.

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:06.400
Anyway, that's what
the app's going to do.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:09.410
I picked Swift because I don't
think Swift is really used

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:10.660
a whole lot in production yet.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:12.580
And I kind of wanted to
emphasize one of those points

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:14.560
earlier that with
containers you really get

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:16.120
a lot of flexibility.

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:18.370
I love Swift, but personally
I've really just not seen

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:19.645
too much being in production.

00:23:19.645 --> 00:23:21.520
So if you do want to
use Swift in production,

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:24.820
I dare say that GKE is a
good place for you to do it.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.820
All right, so what does
this little app do?

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:31.370
This little app, it's using
a library called Swifter.

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:33.910
I'm just going to start
like a basic HTTP server,

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:35.630
handle one endpoint.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:38.018
I'm going to parse a
couple of parameters.

00:23:38.018 --> 00:23:40.060
We're going to convert a
latitude and a longitude

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:44.300
coordinate into a plus code.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:47.260
And then we're going
to return that as JSON.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:48.730
Well, let's just
take a quick look

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:53.560
at what that code looks like.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:56.745
I'm going to build that
container real quick.

00:23:56.745 --> 00:23:58.870
And since it's actually
cached, it should hopefully

00:23:58.870 --> 00:23:59.495
be fairly fast.

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:03.520
All right.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:06.070
And let me run that locally.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:08.350
And so I'm going to forward
port 8080 on my machine

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:10.528
to port 80 in the
container there.

00:24:10.528 --> 00:24:12.070
Let me show you what
that looks like.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:16.770
All right.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.062
So this is the little endpoint.

00:24:18.062 --> 00:24:20.050
And that's using
my example data.

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:23.445
So to prove to you that this
is real, let me pull up some--

00:24:23.445 --> 00:24:25.990
an actual latitude and
longitude to throw in there.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.980
Let's look at the
Moscone Center.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:31.190
I'm just going to use
Wikipedia for this,

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:36.130
just a good source of
geographical coordinates.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.230
So let me grab those--

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.230
whack it in here.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:44.097
And then this is the plus
code for the Moscone Center.

00:24:44.097 --> 00:24:46.680
So if you have Google Maps, you
can type this into Google Maps

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:48.157
and you can find where we are.

00:24:48.157 --> 00:24:50.490
And when I-- actually, one
of the cool things about this

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.573
is you can actually drop
off the first four digits

00:24:52.573 --> 00:24:55.350
if you're in the same region.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:58.140
So anyway, that's my Swift
app running in a container,

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.650
running locally.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.420
Let's get it up onto Kubernetes.

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:06.200
So on Kubernetes, on GKE you
can create a cluster just

00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:07.690
with a few clicks.

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:09.970
I'll show you what
that looks like.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:12.140
You just tap that
create cluster button

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:14.090
and then we have a
whole list of templates

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:16.132
that you can pick from,
so just pick the one that

00:25:16.132 --> 00:25:17.720
matches your workload the best.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:19.452
So say for this demo,
frankly speaking,

00:25:19.452 --> 00:25:21.410
this little-- your first
cluster would probably

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.377
be quite sufficient.

00:25:23.377 --> 00:25:24.710
I can leave all that as default.

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:26.093
Click Create.

00:25:26.093 --> 00:25:28.510
Now, since that might take a
couple of minutes to come up,

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.385
I'm just going to use
this one that I created

00:25:30.385 --> 00:25:32.058
earlier, which is Next Demo.

00:25:32.058 --> 00:25:33.600
So let's take a look
at that cluster.

00:25:36.523 --> 00:25:37.940
And I can click
the Deploy button.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.615
And it's going to ask me
for a container image.

00:25:45.615 --> 00:25:46.990
So let's get back
to the console.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:53.480
And I'm going to tag that image.

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:56.270
And this is using a special tag
for Google Container Registry,

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:59.830
which is GCI.IO and
then my project name,

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:04.072
and then my application name.

00:26:04.072 --> 00:26:05.280
So I'm going to push that up.

00:26:12.747 --> 00:26:14.580
And, of course, looks
like my cache is gone,

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:17.030
so we're going to test how
good my wide connection is

00:26:17.030 --> 00:26:19.948
to upload a gigabyte of data.

00:26:19.948 --> 00:26:21.490
While we're waiting
for it to happen,

00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:25.390
let me just show you a
couple of things here.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:29.940
So Kubernetes-- we
show you the workloads

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:31.650
and the services
and any applications

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:34.220
you're running all in
the UI, which I think

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:37.310
is something that maybe isn't
available on other platforms

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:38.250
yet.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:41.192
So for example, you can go
in, look at the services

00:26:41.192 --> 00:26:41.900
that are running.

00:26:46.805 --> 00:26:47.930
We'll see how that's going.

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:48.170
All right.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:48.962
About halfway done.

00:26:51.593 --> 00:26:53.510
OK, so while we're waiting
for that to upload,

00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:54.995
let me let me cue
up some changes.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:26:59.970
So after I've deployed that,
one thing I'm going to show you

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:02.170
is, also, how to do an update.

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.230
So the first update
I'm going to do

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:06.150
is I'm going to just
make a very small change

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:07.108
in this little service.

00:27:07.108 --> 00:27:09.500
Let's add like a status field.

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:11.717
Like this one.

00:27:11.717 --> 00:27:13.550
So let me queue that
up while we're waiting.

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:24.620
All right.

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:28.640
It looks like we are good.

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:29.310
OK.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.225
So I'll go back to that
deployment that I was creating.

00:27:32.225 --> 00:27:34.350
And I'm going to pick that
image that I just pushed

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:35.558
to Google Container Registry.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:40.860
I can leave the
command as default,

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:42.730
because it's just going to pick
up the command from the Docker

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:43.230
file.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:46.770
I'm going to give that a name.

00:27:51.193 --> 00:27:52.610
Going to pick my
cluster, which is

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.460
going to be that demo cluster.

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:55.430
And just hit Deploy.

00:27:57.952 --> 00:27:59.910
So this has now created
a Kubernetes deployment

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.310
all via the UI.

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:04.170
I can also take a look
from the command line

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:08.280
and see how that deployment is.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:10.288
So I do this cube CTL get pods.

00:28:10.288 --> 00:28:12.330
We're just basically doing
the same thing, but on

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:14.550
the command line,
just inspecting

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:16.280
the state of those pods.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.960
And we can see that
already we have

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:23.350
three containers, three pods
running with that container.

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:26.253
So you can see just
how fast that is.

00:28:26.253 --> 00:28:27.920
Now, the next thing
I'll need to do here

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:32.070
is expose this as a service.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:34.170
So I'm going to go expose.

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:37.190
I'm going to leave that as port
80, because that's my service.

00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:39.980
Pick a load balancer, so we can
access it from the internet.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.980
And click Expose.

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.880
Now, that action will
take about 30 seconds

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:46.190
to complete because it's
actually provisioning a Google

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:49.040
anti-cast IP, one of
various points of presence

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:50.910
around the world.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:52.430
So to save time
there, I've actually

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.188
prepared that one earlier.

00:28:55.188 --> 00:28:56.915
So we'll go to the
Services tab here.

00:28:56.915 --> 00:28:59.040
And we can see this service
that I created earlier,

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:01.740
and just take a look at that.

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:03.530
And so that is now
the same application,

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:06.243
but running in Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:29:06.243 --> 00:29:08.160
Let me pick another
coordinate to try out just

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:09.730
so you know I'm not faking it.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:13.875
Let's look at, say,
the Golden Gate Bridge.

00:29:13.875 --> 00:29:15.094
Using Wikipedia again.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:23.200
Grab that latitudinal,
longitudinal coordinate.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:29.233
OK.

00:29:29.233 --> 00:29:31.150
And I said I would also
demonstrate an update,

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:34.740
so let's go back to the console.

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:36.350
We'll build the container again.

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:40.180
This time tag it as version two.

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:42.180
Fortunately, this one
should be quick to upload.

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:48.330
Sorry.

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:49.580
I need to actually build that.

00:30:06.365 --> 00:30:06.880
OK.

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:07.730
Build it first.

00:30:14.345 --> 00:30:15.720
And while we're
waiting for that,

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:18.240
let me get back into our
Deployment View here.

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.352
And I'll show you how to update.

00:30:20.352 --> 00:30:22.310
So I can just go into
the deployment I created.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:31.245
And I can use this update
action here, rolling update.

00:30:31.245 --> 00:30:33.620
And I'll specify that same
image tag that I just created.

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:39.980
And I can also customize
just how quickly

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.240
I want this thing to
roll out, so by default,

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:45.170
when Kubernetes does a rolling
update, it will one by one

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:48.080
replace the pods
in the deployment

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:50.938
and it will do it in such a
way that there's no downtime.

00:30:50.938 --> 00:30:53.480
If I really wanted that to go
out quickly, I can kind of just

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:55.840
say, you know what, I just want
everything to go out at once.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:56.840
So we can try that here.

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:00.560
OK.

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:03.560
So now that container's
built, let me push it up,

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.060
and then I'll hit
the Update button.

00:31:12.557 --> 00:31:14.140
One really nice thing
about Kubernetes

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:16.300
is, even if I
clicked that update

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:18.400
button before the
container was ready,

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.150
it will actually
handle that condition.

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:21.790
So it would actually
try and pull that image,

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:23.440
it would notice that
it wasn't present,

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:25.492
and it would try again
a few seconds later.

00:31:25.492 --> 00:31:27.200
That's all part of
that operative pattern

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:29.320
I saying where Kubernetes
is driving the desired

00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:32.532
state towards--

00:31:32.532 --> 00:31:34.990
sorry, it's driving the observed
state towards your desired

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:37.600
state.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:38.320
All right.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:40.910
And if I go back in
here, I should now

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:44.534
see we have a new version
of the application running

00:31:44.534 --> 00:31:46.010
just a few seconds ago.

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:54.420
.imagename.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:54.920
OK.

00:31:58.298 --> 00:31:59.340
That would be the reason.

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:06.990
And looking at the
UI view here, we

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.870
see that revision
three of my deployment

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:12.390
is currently in the
container creating state,

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:14.820
so very shortly that will
be available for use.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:19.030
I can inspect the same
thing on the console.

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:21.790
And looks like it might
actually be running.

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:24.620
So let's get back
to our example.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:28.300
Refresh that, and you
see we have that status.

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:31.660
So that's how to deploy and
then update your application

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:33.410
in Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:36.425
I'm going to show one
more thing real quick,

00:32:36.425 --> 00:32:37.800
and that is how
to set up a cloud

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:42.000
build continuous
integration pipeline.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:46.150
So let's go to Cloud
Build and let's

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:48.040
also open up the
source repository.

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:51.640
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to push my application up

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:55.533
to the git repository
in the cloud.

00:32:55.533 --> 00:32:57.950
And I'm then going to use a
continuous integration process

00:32:57.950 --> 00:32:59.822
to build that container.

00:32:59.822 --> 00:33:01.530
So first thing, I'll
create the git repo.

00:33:06.162 --> 00:33:07.370
And I'll call that plus code.

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:15.690
I'll go back to the app.

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.315
I'm going to make
one more change, just

00:33:17.315 --> 00:33:18.940
so we can observe that change.

00:33:18.940 --> 00:33:23.240
Let's say the status
is now awesome.

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:24.890
And thank you for laughing.

00:33:27.970 --> 00:33:28.470
All right.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:29.510
Let's commit that.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:40.340
Well, actually, before I push,
let me create a build trigger.

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:44.863
So now I'm going to Cloud
Build, into the triggers,

00:33:44.863 --> 00:33:47.030
and I'm going to add a
trigger, so that anytime code

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.880
is pushed to that
repository I just created,

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:52.520
it's going to build the
image, the container image

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:54.820
in that repository.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:57.680
So I can actually leave
this all as the default,

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:01.520
and it will actually build
my Docker image for me.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:02.660
So now let me push that up.

00:34:07.100 --> 00:34:08.909
And if we go to
the history here,

00:34:08.909 --> 00:34:10.730
we'll see that a build
has just kicked off.

00:34:15.688 --> 00:34:17.480
And, again, this is
going to take a minute.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.980
But one nice thing is I already
know what the image name is,

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:23.350
so I can grab that.

00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:25.100
And I can do the rolling
update right now,

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:27.478
even before it's ready.

00:34:27.478 --> 00:34:30.020
So I'm just putting the image
name here from the Cloud Build.

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:34.750
In this case, I'm
going to say, I

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:37.150
want a max unavailable
of zero, so I

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:39.909
don't want Kubernetes to
take down any containers

00:34:39.909 --> 00:34:42.543
until the new one is ready.

00:34:42.543 --> 00:34:43.960
I will do a surge
of three though,

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:46.252
because I think there's enough
capacity to handle that.

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:48.540
All right.

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:49.915
So two things are
happening here.

00:34:49.915 --> 00:34:53.070
Kubernetes is actually waiting
for that container to appear

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:57.480
and we're waiting for that
container to finish building.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:59.760
Looks like it is underway.

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:05.960
All right.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:08.460
Well, I think we have waited
enough for me to upload things.

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:10.300
So if you really want,
you can grab that IP

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.600
and check it out later, and
you should see my deployment

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:15.493
from Cloud Build.

00:35:15.493 --> 00:35:17.410
With that, I'll go back
to the slides, please.

00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:21.430
All right.

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:23.100
So one final word
about plus codes,

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.660
if you thought that was
interesting technology,

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:27.107
take a look at plus.codes.

00:35:27.107 --> 00:35:29.190
This is a little project
that a bunch of Googlists

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:31.348
work on in their 20% time.

00:35:31.348 --> 00:35:32.390
I think it's pretty cool.

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:34.050
There's also a
video to check out.

00:35:34.050 --> 00:35:36.217
And I'll be putting the
code that I used in the demo

00:35:36.217 --> 00:35:37.160
online later as well.

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:38.910
That will be the link
at the bottom there,

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:39.990
where I'll upload it.

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:41.573
It's not actually
available quite yet,

00:35:41.573 --> 00:35:44.900
but I'll make sure that's
up by the end of the week.

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:46.410
So what are the next steps?

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:48.530
So now that we've got
our application deployed

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:51.233
into Kubernetes
by the UI, there's

00:35:51.233 --> 00:35:52.650
certainly a lot
of things that you

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.780
might want to do before
you're ready to hit the go

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:57.295
to prod button on that.

00:35:57.295 --> 00:35:59.670
The first one to consider
would be readiness and liveness

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:00.660
checks.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:03.820
So these are probes that you can
configure in Kubernetes where

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:06.053
Kubernetes continuously
probes your container

00:36:06.053 --> 00:36:07.470
to ensure that
it's actually still

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:10.530
running and ready to traffic.

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:13.170
Then, I think you want to
look at requests and limits.

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:17.000
This is specifying the resources
that your container will need.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.010
Requests relate to
how much resources

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.410
get reserved when your
container is scheduled

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:24.110
and limits is the maximum
of those resources

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:25.705
that your container can use.

00:36:25.705 --> 00:36:27.830
So when your limits are
greater than your requests,

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:29.630
it means that your
container can actually

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:33.490
use any unutilized
resources on that note.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.388
Thirdly, I'd recommend looking
at configuration as code.

00:36:36.388 --> 00:36:38.680
So you saw me just deploying
things and updating things

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:40.660
by the UI, which is
a really great way

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:43.000
to get started and
learn Kubernetes.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.927
But eventually, you want
to get that configuration,

00:36:45.927 --> 00:36:48.010
you want to download it
into your version control,

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:49.810
and treat it just
like source code.

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:51.880
So have any
configuration changes

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:55.860
go through code review and any
kind of security process you

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:58.140
have with your source code.

00:36:58.140 --> 00:37:01.150
And through that you'll also
gain the ability to rollback.

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:05.040
So if you released a version
by changing the configuration

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:07.803
through git, you didn't
realize it was a mistake, well,

00:37:07.803 --> 00:37:10.470
you can just do a git revert and
your configuration would revert

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:12.600
and Kubernetes will update.

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:15.360
Finally, I would recommend
looking at auto scaling.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:19.230
So GKE in particular has
a really good auto scaling

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:19.830
technology.

00:37:19.830 --> 00:37:21.988
I actually demoed one
last year at Next.

00:37:21.988 --> 00:37:23.530
And I've put the
link up here for you

00:37:23.530 --> 00:37:25.920
if you're interested to see it.

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:27.480
You can actually
set up a request

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:30.210
per second based pod
order scale, which

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:31.830
is really kind of cool stuff.

00:37:31.830 --> 00:37:35.670
Basically lets you say, for
every 100 simultaneous requests

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:37.170
per second that
I'm getting, I want

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:40.200
to have like one pod backing
that, so if you get 1,000,

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:41.430
I want 10 pods.

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:44.040
That is something that you
can just basically state in

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:45.830
like three lines
of configuration,

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:48.380
and Google Kubernetes Engine
will make that happen for you.

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:51.380
All right.

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:53.840
So with that, I'd like to
invite Cat and Marshall back up

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:55.700
on stage, where
we're going to have

00:37:55.700 --> 00:37:58.070
a bit of a conversation
about rolling out Kubernetes

00:37:58.070 --> 00:38:00.620
to your enterprise.

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:03.732
Cat and Marshall.

00:38:03.732 --> 00:38:04.232
Thanks.

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:12.450
So one thing we realized, as we
were preparing for this talk,

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:16.470
is your experience with
Kubernetes is kind of, I guess,

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:18.720
fairly similar to some other
customers I've spoken to,

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.060
particularly the way that you
actually deploy Kubernetes

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:22.780
into your organization.

00:38:22.780 --> 00:38:25.410
So I thought it might
be interesting to just

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:27.900
have the audience hear from
you sort of how you introduced

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:28.858
Kubernetes to your org.

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:35.650
CATHERINE CAI: Well, yeah, so in
terms of introducing Kubernetes

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:39.460
into your organization and
how quickly you should do it,

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:43.392
I would say very slowly,
like glacially slowly,

00:38:43.392 --> 00:38:45.100
especially if you have
a lot of workloads

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:48.220
that are already in production
that aren't on Kubernetes.

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:50.560
For us, we had the
benefit of being

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:52.300
able to start on Kubernetes.

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:55.440
But, I think, for
most companies,

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:00.480
Kubernetes is a huge thing
to bite into, I guess.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:02.280
And so probably the
best thing to do

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:06.690
is to cut over non-critical,
maybe new apps,

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:08.550
into your Kubernetes
cluster and start

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:10.770
developing in-house
expertise before you

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.493
go whole hog into it.

00:39:12.493 --> 00:39:13.410
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:19.170
Also, we had basically a
year to develop in private,

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:22.270
and more importantly,
fail a lot in private.

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:25.690
So that was sort of why we
were able to start with it.

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:28.830
But as Cat said,
start slowly with kind

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:30.900
of non-critical
things, because it's

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:34.340
easy to make mistakes like that
demo showed, where you roll out

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:36.090
your change and you've
got the image wrong

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:38.548
and you didn't set any safety
and suddenly your application

00:39:38.548 --> 00:39:39.580
is entirely down.

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:41.830
WILLIAM DENNISS: And so
readiness checks for that one,

00:39:41.830 --> 00:39:42.606
right?

00:39:42.606 --> 00:39:43.523
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah.

00:39:43.523 --> 00:39:45.288
Readiness checks.

00:39:45.288 --> 00:39:47.580
WILLIAM DENNISS: And I'm kind
of curious to learn like,

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:49.660
what average level of
Kubernetes knowledge

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:51.100
does it involve to have?

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:55.240
So, for example, does the
typical application developer

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:57.070
need to know about
services and deployments

00:39:57.070 --> 00:40:00.473
and yaml files and things
like that in a daily job?

00:40:00.473 --> 00:40:01.390
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah.

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:04.690
I think for us, most
developers are kind of

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:07.810
at an observability
point of their knowledge.

00:40:07.810 --> 00:40:09.907
They still are doing
some configuration

00:40:09.907 --> 00:40:11.740
at sort of the deployment
and service level,

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:14.423
but it's pretty standardized
in terms of like that's

00:40:14.423 --> 00:40:15.340
all they need to know.

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:16.810
There's a whole bunch
of other abstractions

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:19.143
that Kubernetes provides that
they don't really need to.

00:40:19.143 --> 00:40:21.975
So I think we have some
power users, obviously,

00:40:21.975 --> 00:40:22.600
who are doing--

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:24.520
I think our data
science team, honestly,

00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:26.945
is on the further side
of that power user side.

00:40:26.945 --> 00:40:28.820
And they're doing a lot
of interesting stuff.

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:30.070
And they really
are in the weeds.

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:31.570
But for most people,
it's just like,

00:40:31.570 --> 00:40:33.130
let the pipeline
take care of it,

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:37.120
and then use-- keep GTL to
see, what does that actually

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:37.680
look like?

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:40.163
What's your state
of your deployments?

00:40:40.163 --> 00:40:41.080
WILLIAM DENNISS: Yeah.

00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:44.050
I remember when you said that,
I really kind of like that setup

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:46.510
where, if I'm a developer,
I can kind of not

00:40:46.510 --> 00:40:48.790
use any of the Kubernetes
if I don't want to

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:50.595
and just focus on my container.

00:40:50.595 --> 00:40:51.970
Or if I really
need to understand

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:53.553
how things are working
under the hood,

00:40:53.553 --> 00:40:55.370
I have that level
of access as well.

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:57.078
And I think that kind
of, to me at least,

00:40:57.078 --> 00:40:59.680
contrasts as to a PaaS, where
you sort of can't get access

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:01.785
to that lower layer, right?

00:41:01.785 --> 00:41:04.520
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah.

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:07.490
WILLIAM DENNISS: And so
we talked about failure.

00:41:07.490 --> 00:41:13.330
What tips do you have for
making workloads more resilient?

00:41:13.330 --> 00:41:16.860
CATHERINE CAI: Just we actually
set anti-affinity policies,

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:20.400
so that basically means
telling Kubernetes,

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:23.580
hey, I actually want to
schedule pods of one deployment

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:25.262
onto different nodes.

00:41:25.262 --> 00:41:27.720
This is actually one of those
moments where it's like, yes,

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.240
you should actually fail
privately, spend some time

00:41:30.240 --> 00:41:32.448
really learning Kubernetes,
because this is something

00:41:32.448 --> 00:41:34.825
that we weren't really
aware of, and we actually--

00:41:34.825 --> 00:41:36.450
it became like a war
story in the sense

00:41:36.450 --> 00:41:39.300
that, whoops, we
screwed this up.

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:41.280
We were actually
rolling over a cluster,

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:44.590
bringing in new machines,
and this happens very rarely,

00:41:44.590 --> 00:41:48.630
but we had all of the pods of
one deployment on one machine

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:51.040
and when that machine was
drained and it was taken down,

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:54.262
we actually took down that
entire deployment and service.

00:41:54.262 --> 00:41:56.220
WILLIAM DENNISS: So
basically, with Kubernetes,

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:57.780
if you don't
actually specify how

00:41:57.780 --> 00:42:00.360
you want your pods
kind of spread out,

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:03.498
you could be very unlucky and
have them all land on one node,

00:42:03.498 --> 00:42:04.290
so that's kind of--

00:42:04.290 --> 00:42:06.060
MARSHALL BREKKA: I mean, yeah,
it'll just do a best guess

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:07.518
and try and fit
where it can, which

00:42:07.518 --> 00:42:12.167
is great in terms of filling up
your VMs as best as possible.

00:42:12.167 --> 00:42:14.250
But unless you give it
those hints, it's like, oh,

00:42:14.250 --> 00:42:16.100
I'll put them all on one place.

00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:18.558
WILLIAM DENNISS: And so since
you set up the anti-affinity,

00:42:18.558 --> 00:42:20.140
how's it working?

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:21.740
MARSHALL BREKKA:
Anti-affinitively.

00:42:27.748 --> 00:42:29.540
WILLIAM DENNISS: And
I'm kind of interested

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:31.370
in your journey,
particularly the sense

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:33.650
that you started unmanaged.

00:42:33.650 --> 00:42:35.893
Did you want to elaborate
any more about that?

00:42:35.893 --> 00:42:37.060
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah, sure.

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:42.050
I mean, obviously, as Cat
said, we started on ADBS,

00:42:42.050 --> 00:42:44.660
and there weren't
options for us there.

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:47.890
But it wasn't
necessarily impossible

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:50.835
for us to run it ourselves, so
we said cool, let's go do it.

00:42:50.835 --> 00:42:52.210
But it wasn't
really easy either.

00:42:52.210 --> 00:42:55.120
And speaking very frankly,
it's basically just

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:58.040
the two of us who have that
institutional expertise,

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:01.690
and we're not seeing the
benefits that we really--

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:04.370
well, we're not seeing benefits
from having us just run it,

00:43:04.370 --> 00:43:04.870
we're not--

00:43:04.870 --> 00:43:06.820
like she said, we're
not giving up control

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:09.020
if we go with a
managed offering.

00:43:09.020 --> 00:43:12.880
So that's really where keeping
up with the pace of Kubernetes

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:15.340
really, because there's
features coming in all the time,

00:43:15.340 --> 00:43:17.798
and we want to be able to take
advantage of those features.

00:43:17.798 --> 00:43:20.830
But then if it's like, we got
to do those Kubernetes version

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:23.380
upgrades and version roll-outs,
and that's just more burden

00:43:23.380 --> 00:43:25.150
that we have to take on.

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:30.570
CATHERINE CAI: And just one
additional point to that,

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:31.470
I think--

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:34.860
I was trying to test out
istio, and it probably

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:37.320
took a week to get it
functioning just right.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:40.020
We do have kind of a
bespoke multi-cluster setup,

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:43.200
so it took a little more time
to tweak everything and make

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:44.060
it work.

00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:46.600
But on GKE it's a
one click deploy,

00:43:46.600 --> 00:43:49.455
so that was really cool to
find out after the fact.

00:43:53.065 --> 00:43:54.190
WILLIAM DENNISS: All right.

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:56.732
And the last question from me,
before we throw it open to you

00:43:56.732 --> 00:43:59.040
all, tell me about preemptibles.

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:01.110
So preemptible is,
if you're not aware,

00:44:01.110 --> 00:44:04.210
it's this machine type that
isn't guaranteed to even

00:44:04.210 --> 00:44:07.150
be available and can kind of
be preempted and reclaimed

00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:09.237
with like 30 seconds notice.

00:44:09.237 --> 00:44:11.320
But you mentioned in our
prep that you're actually

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:13.487
using them, so I'm kind
of curious to learn more.

00:44:13.487 --> 00:44:14.320
CATHERINE CAI: Yeah.

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:16.540
For actually all of our
non-production environments

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:19.450
and our data science clusters
we actually use preemptibles.

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:22.318
For data science, it's
actually a great advantage,

00:44:22.318 --> 00:44:23.860
because we're actually
spending cents

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:27.860
on the dollar for those very,
very beefy machine workloads.

00:44:27.860 --> 00:44:31.390
And then all of our staging
and sandbox environments

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:32.705
are using preemptibles.

00:44:32.705 --> 00:44:35.080
And there's actually like a
really funny story associated

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:37.300
with it because
preemptibles are called

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:39.130
preemptibles for a reason--

00:44:39.130 --> 00:44:42.080
and we-- this
happens very rarely,

00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:45.220
but in the two years that we've
been running with preemptibles

00:44:45.220 --> 00:44:47.890
in this setup, we
actually, on a weekend

00:44:47.890 --> 00:44:50.930
once, got a ping from a
developer who was like, hey,

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:55.750
I don't see any pods
in staging, is it down?

00:44:55.750 --> 00:44:58.630
And so we all, we're like,
oh, no, what's going on?

00:44:58.630 --> 00:44:59.950
We like jump in.

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:02.090
You do QCCL get pods,
you see nothing.

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:03.340
And you're like, OK.

00:45:03.340 --> 00:45:05.480
QCCL get nodes, you
also see nothing.

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:08.420
And you're like, OK,
cool, what's going on?

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:11.320
And then it turns
out that there were

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:16.780
none of the instances left to
bid on for those preemptibles.

00:45:16.780 --> 00:45:20.180
And so we just kind
of changed VM types.

00:45:20.180 --> 00:45:22.600
And then staging came
back up in five minutes,

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:25.388
but that is the risk that
you incur doing preemptibles.

00:45:25.388 --> 00:45:26.680
So don't do that in production.

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:28.573
For staging it's fine.

00:45:28.573 --> 00:45:29.490
WILLIAM DENNISS: Yeah.

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:31.090
I think any discussion
of preemptibles

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:32.465
has to come with
that disclaimer,

00:45:32.465 --> 00:45:33.478
don't do in production.

00:45:33.478 --> 00:45:35.020
But it's interesting
because you have

00:45:35.020 --> 00:45:37.690
like non-production workloads
that are still kind of very

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:40.285
important to the business, like
machine learning even, that

00:45:40.285 --> 00:45:41.910
just don't necessarily
need [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:43.077
completed by a certain time.

00:45:43.077 --> 00:45:43.993
MARSHALL BREKKA: Yeah.

00:45:43.993 --> 00:45:46.360
And I mean, I think
especially with those ones,

00:45:46.360 --> 00:45:48.430
it's important to
remember, as she said,

00:45:48.430 --> 00:45:51.780
once we had changed
the instance type

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:53.770
and our nodes were
able to come back in,

00:45:53.770 --> 00:45:56.110
Kubernetes was just like,
oh, the state of the world

00:45:56.110 --> 00:45:58.840
is not in the desired state,
so I need to put it back

00:45:58.840 --> 00:45:59.960
into the desired state.

00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:02.560
So like with machine learning
and the training models

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:05.178
they were doing there, if
the nodes just went away,

00:46:05.178 --> 00:46:07.220
and then eventually they
came back, it'd be like,

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:09.250
oh, there's still
workloads I need to finish.

00:46:09.250 --> 00:46:11.360
And then it would go
and make them happen.

00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:14.170
So that was kind of like
a pseudo disaster recovery

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:14.953
kind of scenario.

00:46:14.953 --> 00:46:15.870
WILLIAM DENNISS: Yeah.

00:46:15.870 --> 00:46:20.410
I definitely think using
preemptibles without Kubernetes

00:46:20.410 --> 00:46:22.030
would be really, really hot.

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:24.190
You basically build
Kubernetes, I think.

00:46:24.190 --> 00:46:26.980
So anyway, cool.

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:29.473
Well, with that, that's the
end of our prepared content.

00:46:29.473 --> 00:46:31.390
But it looks like we
have about three minutes,

00:46:31.390 --> 00:46:32.692
if you have any questions.

00:46:32.692 --> 00:46:33.900
And there's also been a Dory.

00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:35.775
So, I believe, if there's
any Dory questions,

00:46:35.775 --> 00:46:38.330
we'll have those now as well.

00:46:38.330 --> 00:46:41.380
But thank you, everyone,
for joining us today.

00:46:41.380 --> 00:46:43.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:45.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

